-## Subtopics
-
-### NeRF Volumetric Rendering (Coarse/Fine Sampling)
-Uses hierarchical sampling with coarse and fine passes to optimize computational cost. Choose when scene complexity varies significantly and adaptive sampling is beneficial. [Link](979-nerf-volumetric-rendering-coarse-fine-sampling.md)
-
-### Iterative Ray Marching with Neural Volume Inference
-Iteratively refines the ray marching path or density estimates. Choose when high-frequency details require adaptive refinement. [Link](1494-iterative-ray-marching-with-neural-volume-inference.md)
-
-### DVGO Volume Rendering Forward Pass
-Uses a differentiable voxel grid (DVGO) representation instead of a pure MLP. Choose for faster inference at the cost of memory. [Link](2363-dvgo-volume-rendering-forward-pass.md)
-
-### MLP with Positional Encoding
-The underlying neural network architecture used by the in-shader method. [Link](2416-mlp-with-positional-encoding.md)
-
-### Volumetric Primitive Radiance Field Integrator
-Integrates radiance fields using volumetric primitives. [Link](2536-volumetric-primitive-radiance-field-integrator.md)
-
-### Neural Volume Raycasting Debug Visualization
-Tools for visualizing the ray marching process and neural network outputs. [Link](2646-neural-volume-raycasting-debug-visualization.md)
-
-### Neural Network Volume Classification
-Classifies volume data using neural networks. [Link](5271-neural-network-volume-classification.md)
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+- Unsupported MLP Width: If the network width is not 16, 32, or 64 for standard networks, or not a multiple of 16 (16*1 to 16*8) for fV-SRN, the renderer prints an error and returns without rendering [S1, unofficial].
+- Incorrect Encoding Method: If `N_FEATURES_PER_LEVEL` is -1, the renderer prints an error indicating an incorrect encoding method for in-shader rendering [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot M)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $M$ is the number of samples per ray. Each sample requires a neural network inference, which is significantly more expensive than a texture lookup.
--   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the neural network size. `TcnnDeviceVolume` and `FvsrnDeviceVolume` store the MLP weights in GPU memory.
--   **Measured Performance:** Performance is heavily dependent on the MLP width and the hardware's FP32/FP16 throughput. Standard widths (16, 32, 64) are optimized. fV-SRN supports widths up to $16 \times 8$.
--   **Strengths:** High-quality rendering with continuous representations; supports complex shading (gradient, single-shade) directly in the shader; no need for pre-computed 3D textures.
--   **Limitations:** Computationally expensive due to per-sample neural inference; limited by MLP capacity for high-frequency details; requires specific network widths (16, 32, 64 or multiples of 16 for fV-SRN) [S1, unofficial].
--   **When to Use:** When high-quality, continuous volume rendering is required and computational resources allow for per-pixel neural inference. Suitable for scientific visualization and high-fidelity rendering.
--   **When Not to Use:** When real-time performance is critical and the scene is simple enough for texture-based rendering; when memory is constrained and the MLP is large.
